\in0,nl,sp0,left\@MagicWand@ Overview
\sp1,right\ Page \%PAGE\
\left
\np
\ctr\_@Welcome to MagicWand@_<*>

@MagicWand@ is a powerful word processing system designed to meet text preparation requirements for all levels of users:

\in5
*  professional writers
*  programmers
*  text editors
*  office administrators
*  clerical staff
*  students
\in0

\ctr\_@SPECIAL PRINTING CAPABILITIES INCLUDE:@_

\in5
*   @Boldface@, _Underline_

*   no Strikeout, but Overprint (c\k-12\o^te)

*   <SUPER>Script

*   >SUB<Script

*   and any combination -- _@MagicWand@_
\in0

On-screen printer-image text formatting commands include
\ctr\@Automatic-Centering@
which  allows text to be automatically centered on a line with a two-stroke  command.

\in35,justc
Margin settings can be changed, and the reformatted text will be displayed on command.

@Selective margination@ permits "cutting" around an illustration.
\in0,left

A wide range of letter quality and draft printers are supported (including Diablo, NEC, Qume, Epson and Olivetti). @MagicWand@ fully utilizes bidirectional printing capability.

Some printers will support @MagicWand@ commands which specify variable line height and variable and alternate character pitch.

(The examples below will not be demonstrated by all printers.)

\rm75
\h19
This is an example of variable pitch. (.H19)
\h15
This is an example of variable pitch. (.H15)
\h12
This is an example of variable pitch. (.H12)
\h11
This is an example of variable pitch. (.H11)
\h10
This is an example of variable pitch. (.H10)
\h12,rm65

\in2,pi-2
* @MagicWand@ is a registered trademark of Small Business Associates, Inc.
\in6,pi0

\v20\This is an example of variable line height. (.V20)
\v18\This is an example of variable line height. (.V18)
\v16\This is an example of variable line height. (.V16)
\v14\This is an example of variable line height. (.V14)
\v12\This is an example of variable line height. (.V12)
\v10\This is an example of variable line height. (.V10)
\v8\This is an example of variable line height. (.V8)
\v6\This is an example of variable line height. (.V6)
\v8,in0

Daisy wheel printers can also be set to "toggle" between the standard 10-pitch and alternate 12-pitch printing:

\in6
This sentence is printed with the normal 10-pitch setting, \cpi12\then toggled to 12-pitch, \cpi10\then back to normal.
\in0

Also, the @OUT@ command allows direct access to printer features such as \out 27,65\alternate color printing\out 27,66\.

\ssa,ssb
To see the embedded commands which produced this text, follow this procedure:

\in7,pi-2
- In response to the basic prompt from your system, enter

\in15
@edit print.tst<RETURN>@

\in7,pi-2
- When the Opening Menu appears on your screen, press @<RETURN>@ to load a document.
\in0,pi0
\ssa<,ssb>

The first thing you will notice is that the text on the screen includes some characters which do not appear on the printed copy. For example:

\in13,pi-3
o  the heading line is identified by a dot command, @.head@  

o  words in @boldface@ are marked by \bf\"@"\bf@\

\in0,pi0
To continue viewing PRINT.TST on your screen, try each of the following commands to scroll through the file:

\in10
@CTRL F@   to view the NEXT full set of lines

@CTRL G@   to view the PRECEDING full set of lines
\in5

NOTE: Hold down the CTRL key while you press the next key.
\in0

We think you will find @MagicWand@ an easy-to-use, essential tool for all your text processing needs. 
\np
